    "topic_id": 418617,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Given a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > and a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > such that the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/5/1b5bfc268e90556df4feb88dbe6bac65df246891.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MA,MB,MC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"117\" height=\"16\" > intersect the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/a/c2af80f0680f9428356964113ca9e3dd3577f324.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC,CA,AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"99\" height=\"16\" > in this order in points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/d/fed096cb12bb4627f07259bd946a58ff827aefa6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D,E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/c/21c1fa17457c16ebb5ce4d408eb858974fb47143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" > respectively. Prove that there are numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/b/b8b4b0e71c9191bacd9ec5082bd6db739a085274.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\epsilon_1, \\epsilon_2, \\epsilon_3 \\in \\{-1, 1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"140\" height=\"18\" > such that:<br>\n<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/f/48f663b39eedfe518ce8e7d69ac037b8f98d3717.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\epsilon_1 \\cdot \\frac{MD}{AD} + \\epsilon_2 \\cdot \\frac{ME}{BE} + \\epsilon_3 \\cdot \\frac{MF}{CF} = 1.\\]\"  width=\"279\" height=\"37\" >",
    "post_canonical": "Given a triangle $ABC$ and a point $M$ such that the lines $MA,MB,MC$ intersect the lines $BC,CA,AB$ in this order in points $D,E$ and $F,$ respectively. Prove that there are numbers $\\epsilon_1, \\epsilon_2, \\epsilon_3 \\in \\{-1, 1\\}$ such that:\n\n\\[\\epsilon_1 \\cdot \\frac{MD}{AD} + \\epsilon_2 \\cdot \\frac{ME}{BE} + \\epsilon_3 \\cdot \\frac{MF}{CF} = 1.\\]",
    "username": "orl",
